I can’t perform a live FDA recall-database search in this environment, so I cannot verify whether **NOTREAL-77** appears in current FDA records.

Even if an FDA search returns no match, that **does not establish that the bottle was unaffected**. A recall lookup may be incomplete because:

- the recall may be listed under the **manufacturer, distributor, product name, strength, dosage form, or NDC**, rather than “naproxen” alone;
- FDA postings may not include every lot detail or may cover only specified lots;
- the product could be subject to a **state, manufacturer, pharmacy, or non-FDA notice**;
- the lot number may be misread, incomplete, or associated with a different product or package size;
- a product issue may not yet have been reported or posted.

Verification should compare the bottle’s **exact product name, strength, dosage form, manufacturer/labeler, NDC, lot number, expiration date, and package size** against the recall notice and the manufacturer’s current recall information. Contact the dispensing pharmacy or manufacturer using contact information obtained independently from the bottle or an official company website. Do not use the product if its identity or recall status is uncertain; changing or stopping medication can carry risks and is a clinician/pharmacist conversation.

Authoritative FDA sources: [FDA Recalls, Market Withdrawals, & Safety Alerts](https://www.fda.gov/safety/recalls-market-withdrawals-safety-alerts) and [FDA Enforcement Reports](https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/ires/). These are lookup resources, not proof that an unmatched lot is safe.

Informational only; not medical advice.

